                        content: "Search for the best time to visit Chiang Mai and you will be told, repeatedly, that it is \"November to February, cool and dry.\" That is true. What most guides bury in a footnote — or leave out entirely, because it is bad for business — is what happens immediately afterwards.\n\nFrom roughly **mid-February through April**, northern Thailand enters what locals simply call **burning season** (*chuang wela phao*). Agricultural fields are cleared by fire, forest fires spread through the surrounding hills, and the smoke has nowhere to go. Chiang Mai sits in a **mountain basin** — the same geography that gives the city its beautiful ringed horizon acts as a lid, trapping particulates over the valley for weeks at a time.\n\nThe result is not a haze. It is one of the most severe seasonal air pollution events on Earth. **In March 2026, Chiang Mai ranked as the world's most polluted city on IQAir's live global rankings multiple times.** On March 30, 2026, it hit an **AQI of 233 with PM2.5 at 188 µg/m³** — roughly twelve times the World Health Organization's recommended 24-hour exposure limit. Hospitals in Chiang Mai and Chiang Rai reported rising numbers of patients with breathing problems.\n\nWe sell tours in Chiang Mai. It would be commercially convenient to gloss over this. But sending someone with asthma, a small child, or an elderly parent into a March smoke event without warning them is not a trade we're willing to make. Here is what you actually need to know.",
