                        icon: "Clock",
                        content: "Burning season is not a fixed calendar event — it depends on the harvest, the rains, and enforcement of burning bans, which vary year to year. But the pattern is consistent:\n\n**Late January** — Usually still clear. Occasional early haze in the last week.\n\n**February** — The turn. The first half is often fine; the second half deteriorates, sometimes sharply. Mid-February is the practical cut-off for a safe booking.\n\n**March — the worst month, without exception.** This is when AQI routinely passes 150 (Unhealthy) and spikes above 300 (Hazardous) on bad days. Mountain views disappear entirely; Doi Suthep can be invisible from the old city. Sunsets go an eerie orange, which photographs beautifully and means your lungs are working hard.\n\n**April** — Still bad through the first half, and April is also the hottest month (38°C+). Relief usually arrives with the pre-monsoon rains in the second half, and **Songkran (April 13–15)** often coincides with the first proper downpours that wash the air clean. Chiang Mai's Songkran is the best in Thailand — but you are gambling on the timing.\n\n**May onwards** — The monsoon arrives and the air is clean again. Green season is genuinely underrated here: the hills turn brilliant, waterfalls run hard, and prices fall.\n\n**Reading the numbers**: AQI under 50 is good; 51–100 moderate; **101–150 unhealthy for sensitive groups**; 151–200 unhealthy for everyone; 201–300 very unhealthy; 300+ hazardous. During March, 150–250 is a normal day, not an outlier.",
